Hi Dave,

Thanks for the series. Went through all 7 patches against the actual
register/bitfield handling; comments below per patch.

1/7 enable refclk and deassert the PHY reset at probe
------------------------------------------------------
> +	ret = clk_get_by_name(dev, "refclk", &k1_phy->refclk);
> +	ret = clk_enable(&k1_phy->refclk);
> +	ret = reset_get_by_name(dev, "phy", &k1_phy->phy_rst);
> +	ret = reset_deassert(&k1_phy->phy_rst);

Ordering matches Linux: refclk is enabled before the "phy" reset is
deasserted, and k1_pcie_phy_enable_resources() fails the probe (via
k1_pcie_phy_probe()'s "return ret") if either step fails, instead of
just warning as the old clk_get_bulk()/reset_get_bulk() path did.
GLOBAL_PHY_RST is correctly dropped from PCIE_CLK_RES_CTRL in
k1_pcie_combo_phy_calibrate() since the "phy" reset now owns that bit.
One thing worth double-checking: on the err_clk path in
k1_pcie_phy_enable_resources(), only clk_disable(&k1_phy->refclk) is
called if reset_get_by_name()/reset_deassert() fails -- that's fine
functionally (probe fails either way), just flagging that it's not a
symmetric "undo everything" unwind, matches common U-Boot practice.

2/7 don't start the combo PHY PLL to calibrate
------------------------------------------------
> -	writel(0x00000B78, regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG);
> -	writel(CFG_FORCE_RCV_RETRY, regs + PCIE_RC_DONE_STATUS);

Removing the PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG/PCIE_PU_PLL_1/PCIE_PU_PLL_2 writes
(refclk mode, FREF_SEL, REF_100_WSSC, SSC_DEP_SEL, and the 0xb78 PLL
start) from k1_pcie_combo_phy_calibrate() matches the Linux flow you
describe: calibration only needs R_TUNE_DONE via PCIE_RCAL_RESULT, not
a running PLL. This also fixes the whole-register overwrites of
PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG and PCIE_RC_DONE_STATUS that 6/7 later converts to
read-modify-write elsewhere -- good that calibration no longer touches
them at all rather than needing the same fix twice.

3/7 restore port A state after calibration
----------------------------------------------
> +	ctrl = readl(apmu + PCIE_CLK_RES_CTRL);
> +	sel = readl(apmu + PMUA_USB_PHY_CTRL0) & COMBO_PHY_SEL;
...
> +	val = readl(apmu + PCIE_CLK_RES_CTRL);
> +	val &= ~(DEVICE_TYPE_RC | APMU_CLK_GATE_MASK);
> +	val |= ctrl & (DEVICE_TYPE_RC | APMU_CLK_GATE_MASK);
> +	writel(val, apmu + PCIE_CLK_RES_CTRL);
> +
> +	k1_combo_phy_sel(k1_phy, sel);

The restore only touches DEVICE_TYPE_RC and APMU_CLK_GATE_MASK (bits
0-5) in PCIE_CLK_RES_CTRL, leaving PCIE_APP_HOLD_PHY_RST cleared as
left by the calibration sequence itself -- consistent with the commit
message ("APP_HOLD_PHY_RST stays clear, as in Linux"). k1_combo_phy_sel()
is called unconditionally with the saved `sel`, so calibration restores
whichever mode port A (PCIe) vs. the USB 3 lane was in before, rather
than assuming PCIe -- addresses the "could restoring clobber port B"
concern: this patch only ever touches port A's own clock/reset/mode
register and COMBO_PHY_SEL, so port B/C (which are separate PCIe
controller instances per the cover letter) aren't affected. The `goto
out` added for the already-calibrated case correctly still runs the
restore path since `out:` is placed before the restore block, not after
it -- confirmed from the diff context.

4/7 program the termination fields properly
-----------------------------------------------
> +		val &= ~AFE_RTERM_REG;
>  		val |= FIELD_PREP(AFE_RTERM_REG, k1_phy_rterm_rx());
> +		val |= EN_RTERM;
...
> +		val &= ~TX_RTERM_REG;
>  		val |= FIELD_PREP(TX_RTERM_REG, k1_phy_rterm_tx());

Field masks are cleared before OR-ing in the new value on both
PCIE_RX_REG1/AFE_RTERM_REG and PCIE_TX_REG1/TX_RTERM_REG, fixing the
bug where leftover reset-state bits would corrupt the calibrated
value. EN_RTERM is added only to the RX_REG1 write, matching the
commit message's "enables the refclock receiver termination" -- correct
since that's a receiver-side feature, not applicable to TX_REG1.

5/7 set CLKSEL on every lane
-------------------------------
> -	val = readl(regs + PCIE_RC_CAL_REG2);
> -	val |= FIELD_PREP(CLKSEL, CLKSEL_24M);
> -	writel(val, regs + PCIE_RC_CAL_REG2);
> +	for (i = 0; i < lane; i++) {
> +		val = readl(regs + PCIE_RC_CAL_REG2 + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +		val &= ~CLKSEL;
> +		val |= FIELD_PREP(CLKSEL, CLKSEL_24M);
> +		writel(val, regs + PCIE_RC_CAL_REG2 + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +	}

Looping over `lane` with PHY_LANE_OFFSET matches the pattern already
used for the RX/TX termination loops just above it in the same
function, so lane 1 of 2-lane ports B/C now gets CLKSEL set instead of
keeping its reset value. Clearing CLKSEL before the FIELD_PREP write
is the right fix for the OR-only bug described. Choosing to mask only
CLKSEL rather than the whole register (unlike Linux's "val &= CLKSEL")
is called out explicitly in the commit message as intentional, to
avoid clobbering other fields of PCIE_RC_CAL_REG2 -- makes sense.

6/7 don't overwrite whole PLL control registers
----------------------------------------------------
> -	val = 0x00000B78;
> -	writel(val, regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG);
> -	writel(val, regs + PHY_LANE_OFFSET + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG);
> +	for (i = 0; i < lane; i++) {
> +		val = readl(regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +		val &= ~CFG_INTERNAL_TIMER_ADJ;
> +		val |= FIELD_PREP(CFG_INTERNAL_TIMER_ADJ, TIMER_ADJ_PCIE);
> +		writel(val, regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +	}
...
> +	for (i = 0; i < lane; i++) {
> +		val = readl(regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +		val |= CFG_SW_PHY_INIT_DONE;
> +		writel(val, regs + PCIE_PU_ADDR_CLK_CFG + PHY_LANE_OFFSET * i);
> +	}

Splitting the old magic 0xb78 write into named-field read-modify-write
(CFG_INTERNAL_TIMER_ADJ = TIMER_ADJ_PCIE, then CFG_SW_PHY_INIT_DONE set
only after CFG_FORCE_RCV_RETRY and the PLL ref/SSC config are in place)
matches the ordering Linux uses and is consistent with how 7/7's
k1_combo_phy_usb3_power_on() programs the same register for USB 3.
Also note this loop now bounds lane programming by `lane` for both
registers, replacing the old unconditional two-register write (which
the commit message says "is written even when the PHY has only one
lane") -- correctly scoped. REF_100_WSSC is cleared alongside FREF_SEL
in PCIE_PU_PLL_1, which looks right to go with 2/7 having removed that
same clear from the calibration path.

7/7 power on the combo PHY in USB 3 mode
---------------------------------------------
> +static int k1_combo_phy_usb3_power_on(struct phy *phy)
...
> +	val = readl(regs + PCIE_PU_PLL_1);
> +	val &= ~(SSC_DEP_SEL | REF_100_WSSC | FREF_SEL);
> +	val |= FIELD_PREP(SSC_DEP_SEL, SSC_DEP_5000PPM);
> +	val |= FIELD_PREP(FREF_SEL, FREF_24M);
...
> +static int k1_pcie_phy_power_on(struct phy *phy)
> +{
> +	struct k1_pcie_phy *k1_phy = dev_get_priv(phy->dev);
> +
> +	if (k1_phy->is_combo && phy->id == PHY_TYPE_USB3)
> +		return k1_combo_phy_usb3_power_on(phy);
> +
> +	return 0;
> +}

Moving the PLL start from .init to .power_on for the USB 3 case, with
the commit message's justification that the DWC3 glue powers the PHY
on only after its own controller clock/reset are up, is a reasonable
sequencing fix for a problem that would otherwise show up as "xHCI
reset never completes" exactly as described. k1_pcie_phy_init() is
correspondingly trimmed to just `return 0` for the USB3 case (the
k1_combo_phy_sel(k1_phy, true) call that used to run in init is moved
into k1_combo_phy_usb3_power_on() instead), so the combo PHY mode
select still happens, just later in the sequence -- consistent.
CFG_FORCE_RCV_RETRY is explicitly cleared here since it's PCIe-only and
could have been left set by the calibration or an earlier boot stage;
good defensive touch given 2/7 and 6/7 both deal with that same bit.
PLL_TIMEOUT_US reuses the existing PCIe timeout constant for the USB 3
wait -- worth a line in the commit message on whether the lock time
budget is known to be the same for both modes, though I have no reason
to think it isn't given the hardware is the same PLL.

Overall this all tracks the Linux driver closely and each patch is
scoped to one specific bug, which makes it easy to review in isolation
and makes sense as 7 separate patches rather than one squash. No
Reviewed-by/Acked-by/Tested-by tags have been given yet. You did follow
up on this thread confirming testing on a second board, an OrangePi
R2S (K1, 2 GiB, booting from eMMC): combo PHY calibrates and switches
to USB 3 with the PLL locking and xHCI starting there as well, though
with nothing plugged in to confirm SuperSpeed enumeration on that board
and PCIe not being exercised there. Given that and the OrangePi RV2
testing from the cover letter, this looks close to ready -- please
send a v2 addressing the small items above (or reply here if you think
they don't need changes), and it would help to also note the R2S
results in the v2 cover letter alongside the RV2 ones.

Thanks,
Leo
